window.BCA_UNIT_ADVANCED_SUBTOPICS = {
  architecture: [
    [
      {title:"Binary to octal and hexadecimal by grouping",notes:"Grouping works because 8=2³ and 16=2⁴. Starting at the binary point, group integer bits toward the left and fractional bits toward the right. Pad only the outside group with zeroes, then replace each group with its octal or hexadecimal digit. This is faster and less error-prone than converting through decimal.",example:"11010110₂ → 011 010 110 → 3 2 6, so it is 326₈; grouping by fours gives D6₁₆.",exam:"Show the grouping boundaries and keep the original base and answer base visible."},
      {title:"Decimal to binary by repeated division",notes:"For a positive whole number, repeatedly divide by 2 and record each remainder. The remainders read from bottom to top form the binary answer. For a fraction, repeatedly multiply the fractional part by 2 and record each integer part from top to bottom. Stop when the fraction reaches zero or when the required precision is reached.",example:"13÷2 gives remainders 1,0,1,1 from bottom upward, so 13₁₀=1101₂.",exam:"Show every quotient and remainder; for fractions, state if the result is rounded."},
      {title:"BCD and binary number codes",notes:"Binary-coded decimal stores each decimal digit in its own four-bit group. It is not the same as converting the whole decimal number to pure binary. BCD makes decimal display and digit-wise arithmetic convenient, but uses more bits than compact binary for many values. Distinguish numeric representation from character encoding such as ASCII.",example:"Decimal 59 is BCD 0101 1001, while pure binary is 111011₂.",exam:"Label the method before converting and check each BCD nibble is a decimal digit 0–9."},
      {title:"K-map don't-care conditions",notes:"A don't-care input combination may be assigned either 0 or 1 when simplifying because that combination never occurs or its output does not matter. Use it only when it helps form a larger legal group; it need not be included. The final expression must still give the required output for every specified input row.",example:"If a 1 is adjacent to a don't-care cell, grouping both can remove a changing variable.",exam:"Mark don't-cares separately, then explain which ones you used to simplify."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Encoders and decoders",notes:"A decoder takes an n-bit code and activates one of up to 2ⁿ output lines, often with enable controls. An encoder performs the reverse kind of task by producing a code for an active input; a priority encoder resolves cases where several inputs are active. Compare the direction of information flow and state the assumptions about active-high or active-low signals.",example:"A 2-to-4 decoder maps input 10₂ to output line 2 as the active line.",exam:"Give input and output counts and identify which output is active for a sample code."},
      {title:"Multiplexers and demultiplexers",notes:"A multiplexer routes one of many data inputs to one output using select bits. A demultiplexer routes one input to one of several outputs. With n select bits there are 2ⁿ possible selections. A MUX can also implement Boolean functions by connecting data inputs to 0 or 1 according to the truth table.",example:"A 4-to-1 MUX needs two select bits; S₁S₀=11 selects I₃.",exam:"Convert the select code to a line number and state the data path."},
      {title:"SR latch versus clocked flip-flop",notes:"An SR latch is level-sensitive: its stored output can respond while its enable level is active. An edge-triggered flip-flop samples at a clock transition. Both store state, but timing behavior differs. The basic SR design also has an invalid input combination, so always state the exact latch or flip-flop convention in a truth table.",example:"A D flip-flop changes Q at the active clock edge; a transparent latch may follow D while enabled.",exam:"Mention whether the device responds to a level or an edge."},
      {title:"Counter modulus and state sequence",notes:"A modulo-M counter repeats after M distinct states. A binary n-bit counter naturally has 2ⁿ states; a custom counter may use reset logic to return earlier. To design or explain one, list the states in order, mark the transition back to the start and check that unused states behave safely if required.",example:"A modulo-6 counter uses 000,001,010,011,100,101, then returns to 000.",exam:"Show the full state sequence and explain how the reset condition is detected."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Arithmetic-logic unit and control unit",notes:"The ALU performs arithmetic and logical operations on operands. The control unit decodes instructions and issues control signals that select registers, ALU functions and memory actions. Together with registers and buses, they form the processor's central datapath. When tracing an instruction, distinguish the unit that transforms a value from the unit that coordinates the sequence.",example:"For ADD R1,R2, control selects the ADD function and routes both register values to the ALU.",exam:"Trace control signals and data movement as separate but coordinated jobs."},
      {title:"Instruction format and instruction length",notes:"An instruction format allocates bits to an opcode and operand fields. A fixed-length format simplifies fetching and decoding; a variable-length format can use space more flexibly but may require more complex boundaries. Field width limits how many opcodes, registers or address values can be represented. Use the format shown in the question rather than assuming a real CPU layout.",example:"A 3-bit register field can name at most eight registers because 2³=8.",exam:"Calculate field capacity using 2^(number of bits) and label each field."},
      {title:"Memory read and write bus cycles",notes:"A memory read places an address on the address path, activates the read control signal and returns the selected contents on the data path. A write places both an address and the value to store, then activates write control. MAR and MDR commonly support these transfers. Timing may require the CPU to wait until memory signals that data is ready.",example:"To store 25 at address 300, put 300 in the address register, 25 on the data path and issue WRITE.",exam:"For each cycle, state address, data direction and read/write control."},
      {title:"Subroutine calls, return addresses and interrupts",notes:"A call transfers control to a procedure and preserves the return address so execution can resume afterward. A stack is a common place to save it. An interrupt is an event that temporarily transfers control to a handler; the processor preserves enough state to resume the interrupted program. Do not confuse a normal procedure call with an external event that requests attention.",example:"A timer interrupt saves the current program location, runs its handler and later returns to that location.",exam:"Show what is saved, where control goes and how the original work resumes."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Virtual memory and address translation",notes:"Virtual memory gives each process a logical address space that the operating system maps to physical memory. Paging divides virtual and physical memory into pages and frames; a page table records the mapping. A page fault occurs when the needed page is not currently in RAM, so the OS fetches it from secondary storage. This supports protection and larger address spaces, but storage access is slower.",example:"A virtual page number plus offset is translated to a frame number plus the same offset.",exam:"Explain page, frame, page table and page fault in the order they are used."},
      {title:"Cache write-through and write-back",notes:"With write-through, a cache update is also sent to main memory, keeping the two copies synchronized but increasing traffic. With write-back, the cache marks a changed block dirty and writes it to memory only when it is evicted. Write-back can reduce traffic but needs extra state and careful handling. A question may also specify write-allocate or no-write-allocate on a miss.",example:"A dirty write-back block is copied to memory when its cache line must be replaced.",exam:"State when memory is updated and what extra behavior the policy requires."},
      {title:"Throughput, latency and pipeline speedup",notes:"Latency is the time for one instruction or task to finish; throughput is how many finish per unit time. Pipelining mainly raises throughput by overlapping stages, while the first result still waits for the pipeline to fill. Ideal speedup is bounded by stage balance and stage count; stalls, branches and unequal stage delays lower the real gain.",example:"A five-stage pipeline may approach one completion per cycle after filling, even though one instruction still visits five stages.",exam:"Use the word throughput for overlap and latency for one task's elapsed time."},
      {title:"Booth multiplication: bit-pair decision table",notes:"Booth's algorithm represents signed two's-complement multiplication compactly. Inspect the current low multiplier bit Q₀ and the extra bit Q₋₁: 00 or 11 means no add/subtract, 01 means add the multiplicand, and 10 means subtract it. Then perform an arithmetic right shift of the combined registers and repeat for the chosen bit width.",example:"For the pair Q₀Q₋₁=10, subtract M before the arithmetic shift.",exam:"Write each pair decision and show the A, Q and Q₋₁ registers after every shift."}
    ]
  ],
  evs: [
    [
      {title:"Land degradation and soil erosion",notes:"Land degradation is a decline in the land's ability to support ecological or human uses. Erosion removes fertile topsoil through water or wind; compaction, salinization and contamination can also reduce soil quality. Deforestation, overgrazing, poor irrigation and construction can accelerate damage. Prevention includes maintaining vegetation, contour practices, careful grazing and restoring soil organic matter.",example:"Plants on a slope slow runoff; removing them can increase soil carried into a river.",exam:"Name the pressure, the soil process and the effect on farming or water."},
      {title:"Watersheds and integrated water management",notes:"A watershed is the land area that drains to a shared stream, lake or outlet. Activities upstream can affect water quantity and quality downstream, so water planning should follow the whole catchment rather than a single well or village boundary. Rainwater harvesting, recharge protection, efficient use and fair allocation can be combined according to local conditions.",example:"Fertilizer washed from fields upstream can increase nutrient levels in a downstream lake.",exam:"Map upstream action → water pathway → downstream user or ecosystem."},
      {title:"Forests, mining and competing resource uses",notes:"Land and resource projects can create jobs, energy, materials or public services while changing habitats and livelihoods. A balanced assessment identifies who receives the benefit, who carries the impact, how long effects last and whether alternatives exist. Restoration and compensation are important, but they may not recreate every cultural or ecological value lost.",example:"A mine can supply minerals while fragmenting habitat and altering local streams.",exam:"Compare benefits and costs across ecological, social and economic dimensions."},
      {title:"Resource management and the precautionary approach",notes:"Resource management sets limits and practices that keep use within ecological and social constraints. Monitoring can reveal early warning signs such as falling groundwater or declining fish catches. When impacts may be serious or irreversible but evidence is incomplete, a precautionary approach favors careful safeguards and further study instead of assuming no harm.",example:"Set a groundwater pumping threshold and reduce withdrawals if monitoring shows the water table falling.",exam:"Explain the monitoring indicator and the action taken when it crosses a limit."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Biomes and ecosystem conditions",notes:"A biome is a broad ecological region shaped by climate, soils and characteristic plant and animal communities. Temperature and rainfall patterns influence which organisms can survive and how ecosystems function. Biomes are not simply lists of species: explain the environmental conditions and adaptations that link the community to its place.",example:"A desert community includes organisms adapted to limited water and large temperature changes.",exam:"Describe climate first, then connect adaptations and ecosystem structure."},
      {title:"Food-chain arrows and trophic roles",notes:"A food-chain arrow points from the organism being eaten toward the organism receiving energy. Producers form the base of most food chains; primary consumers eat producers, while higher consumers feed on other consumers. An organism may occupy different roles in different chains, which is why a food web gives a fuller picture.",example:"Grass → grasshopper → frog means energy moves from grass to grasshopper to frog.",exam:"Label trophic roles and draw arrows in the direction energy moves."},
      {title:"Biodiversity at genetic, species and ecosystem levels",notes:"Biodiversity includes variation within a species, variety among species and diversity among ecosystems. Genetic variation can help populations respond to changing conditions; species diversity supports ecological relationships; ecosystem diversity provides different habitats and services. Conservation at only one level may miss risks at another.",example:"Protecting a wetland preserves habitat diversity while also supporting many species and their genetic populations.",exam:"Name all three levels and apply each to the example in the question."},
      {title:"Ecosystem services and human wellbeing",notes:"Ecosystem services are benefits people obtain from natural systems. Provisioning services include food and materials; regulating services include flood control and climate regulation; cultural services include recreation and cultural meaning; supporting processes include soil formation and nutrient cycling. These categories help explain why an ecosystem has value beyond the products sold from it.",example:"A mangrove can provide fish habitat, reduce some wave impacts and support cultural or livelihood practices.",exam:"Name the service category and explain the process that creates the benefit."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Population, consumption and environmental pressure",notes:"Environmental pressure depends not only on population size but also on consumption patterns, technology and how resources are produced. A small high-consumption group can create large impacts, while communities with low resource use may be highly exposed to environmental harm. Good analysis avoids blaming population alone and examines systems of production, access and waste.",example:"Compare two communities by energy use per person and energy source, not only total population.",exam:"Identify more than one driver and explain how each affects environmental pressure."},
      {title:"Environmental ethics and public participation",notes:"Environmental ethics asks what duties people and institutions have toward other people, future generations and non-human life. Participation allows affected communities to share local knowledge and influence decisions. A fair process needs accessible information, enough time to respond and a clear explanation of how concerns changed the plan.",example:"Fishers may identify seasonal breeding grounds that a one-time survey does not show.",exam:"Connect an ethical principle to a specific decision and affected group."},
      {title:"Pollution-control laws and regulatory roles",notes:"The Water Act, Air Act and Environment Protection Act have broad purposes named in the syllabus, but exact duties depend on current statutory text, rules and amendments. Distinguish legislation from the agencies and standards used to implement it. In an exam, state the act's broad role and use class material for details rather than inventing a penalty or procedure.",example:"A factory's water discharge may be assessed under water-pollution controls and applicable consent requirements.",exam:"Name the law, state its broad scope and avoid unsupported legal specifics."},
      {title:"Climate mitigation and adaptation",notes:"Mitigation reduces greenhouse-gas emissions or increases removals; adaptation reduces harm from climate impacts. The two can work together, but they solve different parts of the problem. Solar energy and efficiency are mitigation actions; heat alerts, water planning and resilient buildings can be adaptation. Assess benefits, costs and access for the people affected.",example:"A cool-roof program can reduce indoor heat exposure and lower cooling-energy demand.",exam:"Label each action as mitigation, adaptation or both and explain why."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Noise and marine pollution pathways",notes:"Noise pollution is unwanted sound that can affect health, learning, sleep and wildlife. Marine pollution includes plastics, oil, chemicals and nutrients entering coastal or ocean systems. Their pathways differ, so a single “pollution control” answer is too broad. Reduce exposure at the source, manage transport routes and monitor the place where harm occurs.",example:"A quiet-zone design near a hospital controls noise at the receiver, while vehicle rules target the source.",exam:"Identify pollutant, pathway, receptor and a control matched to that pathway."},
      {title:"Ozone layer versus greenhouse gases",notes:"Stratospheric ozone absorbs much of the Sun's harmful ultraviolet radiation. Ozone-depleting substances can trigger chemical reactions that reduce this protective layer. Greenhouse gases instead affect how heat is absorbed and emitted in the lower atmosphere. Some chemicals can affect both climate and ozone, but the mechanisms and impacts should be explained separately.",example:"CFCs are linked to ozone depletion; carbon dioxide strengthens the greenhouse effect, through a different process.",exam:"Use a two-column comparison: atmospheric layer, mechanism and main consequence."},
      {title:"Acid deposition and ecosystem effects",notes:"Acid deposition occurs when sulfur and nitrogen pollutants form acidic compounds that return to Earth in rain, snow, fog or dry particles. It can acidify lakes and soils, damage vegetation and corrode materials. Effects depend on local geology and buffering capacity. Controls reduce precursor emissions through cleaner energy and pollution-control equipment.",example:"A lake with low buffering capacity may show a larger pH change after acidic deposition.",exam:"Trace pollutant emission → atmospheric reaction → deposition → ecosystem effect."},
      {title:"Circular economy and waste prevention",notes:"A circular approach designs products and systems to reduce material use, extend product life, repair, reuse and recover materials. It aims to keep value in use and avoid waste at the source. Recycling is useful, but it still needs energy and cannot recover every material perfectly, so prevention and reuse often come earlier in the hierarchy.",example:"Repairing a laptop extends its use and delays mining, manufacturing and disposal impacts.",exam:"Place the action on the waste hierarchy and explain the resources it saves."}
    ]
  ],
  english: [
    [
      {title:"Prefixes, suffixes and spelling changes",notes:"Affixes change a word's meaning or grammatical role. Some endings require spelling changes: a final e may be dropped before a vowel-starting suffix, or a consonant may double in a stressed syllable. These patterns have exceptions, so use a dictionary when unsure. Learning a word family helps you recognize new words but does not guarantee the same meaning in every context.",example:"Create → creation drops the final e; happy → happiness changes y to i.",exam:"Show the base, the spelling change, the suffix and the new word class."},
      {title:"Synonyms in formal and informal contexts",notes:"A synonym is a close meaning match, not always a perfect replacement. Word choice can differ by formality, emotional tone, collocation and grammar. A useful way to test a synonym is to substitute it into a sentence and check whether meaning and tone remain appropriate. Antonyms can also be graded, such as warm/cool, or complementary, such as alive/dead.",example:"“Ask” and “inquire” are related, but inquire is more formal in many settings.",exam:"Use both words in a sentence and describe the difference in shade or register."},
      {title:"Abbreviations, acronyms and initialisms",notes:"An abbreviation shortens a word or phrase. An acronym is usually pronounced as a word, while an initialism is spoken letter by letter. Some course contexts accept common forms such as CPU; unfamiliar shorthand can make a formal answer unclear. Define a technical abbreviation on first use when the reader may not know it.",example:"“CPU” is an initialism; “NASA” is commonly pronounced as a word.",exam:"Distinguish the forms and match the abbreviation to the intended audience."},
      {title:"Context clues for unfamiliar vocabulary",notes:"Surrounding words can suggest an unfamiliar word's meaning through a definition, example, synonym, contrast or cause-and-effect clue. Read the full sentence and the sentence before or after it; then test a possible meaning in context. Context clues help, but a dictionary confirms precise meaning, spelling and usage.",example:"“Unlike his usually talkative sister, Arun was taciturn” signals a contrast with quietness.",exam:"Name the clue in the passage and explain how it supports your meaning."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Sentence types by clause structure",notes:"Sentence type depends on clauses, not sentence length. A simple sentence has one independent clause; a compound sentence has at least two independent clauses; a complex sentence combines an independent clause with a dependent clause; a compound-complex sentence has both relationships. Label clauses before classifying a long sentence.",example:"“I read, and she wrote because the test was near” is compound-complex.",exam:"Underline each finite verb, identify its clause and classify the structure."},
      {title:"Parallel structure in lists and comparisons",notes:"Parallel structure uses the same grammatical form for ideas that play the same role. It makes lists easier to read and comparisons fair. A sentence that mixes a noun, a verb phrase and an -ing phrase can sound uneven. Check each item after words such as and, or, both, either and not only.",example:"Parallel: “The course teaches reading, writing and speaking,” all gerunds.",exam:"Make the compared elements match in grammatical form."},
      {title:"Topic sentences and paragraph evidence",notes:"A topic sentence signals the paragraph's main point and controls which details belong. Evidence may include facts, examples, explanation or a quotation, but it needs interpretation that links back to the point. A paragraph with evidence but no explanation can leave the reader unsure why the detail matters.",example:"Claim: trees reduce heat. Evidence: shaded streets are cooler. Explanation: shade limits direct solar heating of surfaces.",exam:"Use point → evidence → explanation, and keep all three on one main idea."},
      {title:"Comma, semicolon and colon choices",notes:"A comma separates items, introductory material or clauses joined by a coordinating conjunction. A semicolon can link closely related independent clauses. A colon can introduce an explanation or list after a complete clause. Punctuation does not replace sentence structure: check whether the words before a mark form a complete clause.",example:"“The test was challenging; the worked examples helped.” Both sides are complete clauses.",exam:"Identify the clause boundary and explain why the chosen mark fits."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Modifiers and misplaced descriptions",notes:"A modifier should sit close to the word it describes. A misplaced modifier can make a sentence unintentionally funny or unclear; a dangling modifier has no clear word to modify. After writing an opening phrase, check that the next subject is the person or thing performing that action.",example:"Unclear: “Walking to class, the rain soaked Meera.” Better: “Walking to class, Meera was soaked by the rain.”",exam:"Name what the modifier describes and place it beside that word."},
      {title:"Homophones and commonly confused words",notes:"Homophones sound alike but differ in spelling or meaning; other commonly confused pairs look similar but have different grammatical roles. Use the sentence's meaning and grammar to choose among them. Proofreading by sound alone will not catch these errors, so check high-frequency pairs such as their/there/they're and its/it's.",example:"“Its cover is blue” shows possession; “It's blue” means “it is blue.”",exam:"State what each form means and show the correct form in context."},
      {title:"Modifiers, adverbs and precise placement",notes:"Adjectives usually describe nouns; adverbs often describe verbs, adjectives or other adverbs. Place an adverb so the reader can tell which action or quality it modifies. Words such as only can change meaning depending on position, so put them next to the word they limit.",example:"“Only Riya submitted the form” differs from “Riya submitted only the form.”",exam:"Move the modifier and explain the change in meaning."},
      {title:"A complete proofreading sequence",notes:"A reliable edit moves from meaning to mechanics. First check that each response answers the prompt and ideas are ordered. Next inspect sentence boundaries, agreement, pronouns, articles, tense and prepositions. Finally check spelling, capitalization and punctuation. Reading aloud catches rhythm problems; a separate pass for one error type catches patterns more reliably.",example:"First revise a confusing sentence for meaning, then check its verbs, and finally proofread spelling.",exam:"Give the correction and the rule that explains it."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Sensory details and point of view",notes:"Description uses selected visual, sound, smell, taste or touch details to help the reader imagine a scene. Point of view controls what the narrator can know and how close the reader feels to the subject. Choose details that support one impression, and keep the viewpoint consistent rather than listing unrelated sensations.",example:"A first-person account can describe the cold handle and the narrator's hesitation without claiming what another person thinks.",exam:"Identify the viewpoint and select details that reinforce the intended atmosphere."},
      {title:"Definition by example and contrast",notes:"A definition can become clearer when followed by an example and a contrast. The example shows what belongs to the category; the contrast marks its boundary. Avoid examples that are more complicated than the term itself. For technical topics, use a simple but accurate case and name a common non-example.",example:"A compiler translates code before execution; an interpreter typically processes instructions during execution.",exam:"Give the class, defining feature, one example and one useful distinction."},
      {title:"Classification paragraphs and category logic",notes:"A classification paragraph divides a broad subject into groups using one stated principle. Categories should be exhaustive for the scope, non-overlapping where possible and parallel in form. If categories mix different bases—such as purpose, size and date—the reader cannot see the logic. Introduce the rule, explain each group and compare them consistently.",example:"Classify storage by access role: primary storage, cache and secondary storage.",exam:"State one classification basis and check each example fits one category."},
      {title:"Introductions and conclusions that do work",notes:"An introduction should orient the reader and establish the focus; it need not begin with a grand generalization. A conclusion synthesizes the main reasoning and gives the reader a sense of completion. Do not introduce a major new argument only in the conclusion. Match the length and tone to the task.",example:"A short essay introduction defines the issue and states its position; the conclusion draws out the implication.",exam:"Make the opening set up the argument and the ending answer “so what?”"}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Literal questions, inference and evidence",notes:"A literal question asks for information stated directly. An inferential question asks what follows from clues and relationships. A personal-response question invites a view but still expects support from the passage. Read the command carefully, answer in your own words and use only evidence the text supports.",example:"If a character packs an umbrella after checking dark clouds, you may infer rain is expected; do not claim it rained unless stated.",exam:"Separate direct evidence from inference and point to the clue."},
      {title:"Precis tone, person and logical order",notes:"A précis should preserve the writer's central argument while removing secondary examples and repetition. Use your own concise wording, retain the original logical order and keep a neutral tone. Do not add personal judgment, new facts or a conclusion the source does not make. A word limit is part of the task, not a suggestion.",example:"If the source lists three examples of one reason, keep the reason and omit the extra illustrations.",exam:"Check meaning, order, neutrality and word count before submission."},
      {title:"Essay thesis and evidence plan",notes:"A thesis tells the reader the main claim or purpose. An outline turns it into a small number of supporting points, each with evidence and explanation. The plan should answer every part of the prompt and avoid repeating the same reason in several paragraphs. A counterpoint can strengthen an argument when the task permits it.",example:"For a claim about campus recycling, plan separate points on facilities, student habits and measurement.",exam:"Make each body paragraph support one distinct part of the thesis."},
      {title:"Timed revision and final checks",notes:"Under time pressure, reserve a final review rather than writing until the last second. Check that every question part has an answer, paragraph order is logical, sentences are complete and examples are relevant. Correct the errors that most affect meaning first. A short checklist reduces repeated rereading that does not change the work.",example:"Use the final two minutes to check omitted subparts, verb tense and the word limit.",exam:"Prioritize content completion, clarity and high-impact errors."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Vowels, consonants and articulation",notes:"Speech sounds can be described by how the vocal tract produces them. Vowels use relatively open airflow and differ by tongue height, position and lip shape; consonants involve a constriction or closure. Learning the physical contrast can make pronunciation practice more precise than repeating a word without noticing what changed.",example:"For /p/ and /b/, the lips close in the same place, but voicing differs.",exam:"Name the articulation feature and use a minimal pair to demonstrate it."},
      {title:"Syllables and word-stress patterns",notes:"A syllable is a spoken beat organized around a vowel sound. In longer words, one syllable often receives stronger stress through loudness, length or pitch. Stress patterns can distinguish words or make speech easier to follow. Use a trusted pronunciation model because stress is not always predictable from spelling.",example:"“REcord” as a noun and “reCORD” as a verb differ in stress in common pronunciation.",exam:"Mark the stressed syllable and state how stress changes the word or rhythm."},
      {title:"Pauses, rhythm and phrasing",notes:"Pauses group words into meaningful units and give listeners time to process information. English rhythm often gives stronger prominence to content words while reducing some function words, though natural speech varies. Pausing at clause boundaries improves clarity; pausing in the middle of a name or phrase can confuse the listener.",example:"Pause after “When the results arrived,” before stating what happened.",exam:"Mark phrase groups and explain how the pause supports meaning."},
      {title:"Presentation opening, structure and close",notes:"A clear presentation tells listeners its purpose, previews the main points, develops them in an easy order and ends with a summary or next step. Signposting phrases help the audience follow transitions. Slides should support the speaker rather than repeat every spoken sentence. Practise timing and prepare for likely questions.",example:"“Today I will explain the problem, compare two solutions and recommend one” previews a structure.",exam:"Describe audience, purpose, signposts and a suitable conclusion."}
    ]
  ],
  iks: [
    [
      {title:"Rigveda and the four Vedas",notes:"The syllabus names Rigveda, Yajurveda, Samaveda and Atharvaveda. They are distinct Vedic collections associated with different textual materials and functions in traditional classification. Avoid reducing them to one undifferentiated book or making unsupported claims about every passage. Use the course's description and explain why the fourfold classification matters.",example:"A revision table can pair each Veda's name with one feature emphasized in class.",exam:"List all four accurately and include the course-approved distinguishing point."},
      {title:"Caturdasa Vidyasthana as a knowledge map",notes:"Caturdasa Vidyasthana is a traditional classification of fourteen fields of learning. The course may organize the count using the four Vedas, six Vedangas, Purana, Nyaya, Mimamsa and Dharma-related learning; follow the exact formulation taught. Its educational value is that it maps connected disciplines rather than presenting knowledge as a single subject.",example:"A tree diagram can group the Vedas and their supporting disciplines before showing the additional fields.",exam:"State the 14-field scheme exactly as taught and explain the grouping logic."},
      {title:"The 64 Kalas and practical arts",notes:"The 64 Kalas are a traditional classification of arts and practical capabilities. Lists and interpretations can differ across textual contexts, so use the syllabus list rather than implying there is one universal modern curriculum. The concept illustrates a broad view of learning that can include artistic, social and practical skills alongside textual study.",example:"For an exam response, group listed arts into a few categories such as performance, craft and practical knowledge.",exam:"Describe the classification's purpose and give examples from the course list."},
      {title:"Text, commentary and interpretation",notes:"A knowledge tradition may include a root text, explanations, commentaries, debate and teaching practice. A short rule or verse can depend on interpretation and context. When studying a concept, identify whether a claim comes from the base text, a later interpretation or the modern course explanation. This keeps historical discussion precise.",example:"Compare a short aphorism with a commentary that explains how it is applied.",exam:"Name the source layer and avoid presenting one interpretation as the only possible one."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Vyakarana and linguistic analysis",notes:"Vyakarana studies grammar and the structure of language. Panini's rule-based system is one syllabus example of formal analysis: a form can be derived through conditions and operations. This makes language study systematic, but a modern comparison should focus on the shared idea of explicit rules without claiming the systems are identical.",example:"A rule can predict a changed form when a base and suffix combine under a stated condition.",exam:"Explain the rule, the condition and the resulting form."},
      {title:"Measurement, units and mathematical practice",notes:"Scientific work depends on agreed measurement, comparison and calculation. Historical measurement practices were tied to tasks, instruments and local conventions, so units and standards must be described in context. A strong answer distinguishes the measurement problem, the method used and the level of precision available.",example:"A construction task requires consistent length measures so separate parts fit together.",exam:"Name what was measured, how it was measured and why consistency mattered."},
      {title:"Anumana and reasoning from evidence",notes:"Anumana is commonly discussed as inference: reasoning from a sign or observed relation toward a conclusion. A sound inference depends on a relevant connection between evidence and what is inferred. Explain the steps rather than calling every guess an inference. Follow the precise terminology and examples in the course material.",example:"Seeing smoke can support an inference of fire when the context makes that connection reliable.",exam:"State the observed sign, the relation used and the conclusion drawn."},
      {title:"Knowledge, doubt and verification",notes:"A question of knowledge can involve the knower, the object being known, the means of knowing and uncertainty about the claim. Doubt can motivate investigation rather than simply block it. Compare sources of knowledge using the framework taught, and explain what would count as adequate support for a conclusion.",example:"For a claim about a measurement, identify the instrument, observed value and possible source of error.",exam:"Separate the claim from its evidence and mention what could confirm or challenge it."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Place-value calculations in historical mathematics",notes:"Place value lets the position of a digit determine its contribution. A zero placeholder preserves empty positions and supports compact written calculations. When explaining a historical method, show a concrete calculation and use the notation in the course. Do not conflate the historical numeral system with later notation without explanation.",example:"In 4,072, the zero keeps 4 in the thousands place and 7 in the tens place.",exam:"Show place values and explain the zero's role in the written number."},
      {title:"Combinatorics from metre patterns",notes:"A sequence of short and long syllables can be treated as choices across positions. With n positions and two independent choices each, there are 2ⁿ patterns. This counting connection makes prosody a useful setting for combinatorial reasoning. Make clear which historical pattern is being discussed and how the modern counting representation is being used.",example:"Four syllable positions have 2⁴=16 short/long patterns before any metre rules restrict them.",exam:"Define the two choices, count the positions and explain any restriction."},
      {title:"Calendrical cycles and astronomical calculation",notes:"Calendars combine observations and conventions for organizing repeating cycles such as lunar phases, weekdays and seasonal positions. A Panchanga records several measures rather than one simple date. Explain how the measures relate to timekeeping and distinguish observation from the rules used to calculate a calendar entry.",example:"A lunar day and a weekday may differ in how their boundaries are calculated.",exam:"Define each measure and avoid treating calendar terms as interchangeable."},
      {title:"Historical instruments and uncertainty",notes:"An astronomical instrument turns geometry, alignment and observation into a measured reading. Accuracy depends on construction, calibration, atmospheric conditions and the observer's method. Historical instruments can show sophisticated design while still having limits. Name the measured quantity and describe at least one source of uncertainty.",example:"A shadow-based reading can vary with alignment and the exact time of observation.",exam:"Explain instrument principle, output and one practical limitation."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Smelting, forging and heat treatment",notes:"Smelting separates metal from ore using heat and chemical conditions; forging shapes heated metal by force; heat treatment changes properties through controlled heating and cooling. These processes are distinct stages, though a craft process may combine them. Material properties depend on composition and treatment, not simply the object's appearance.",example:"Heating and hammering a metal bar changes its shape; controlled cooling can alter hardness.",exam:"Use process verbs accurately and connect each step to the material outcome."},
      {title:"Alloys and material properties",notes:"An alloy combines a metal with one or more other elements to change properties such as hardness, strength, corrosion resistance or melting behavior. Composition and processing both matter. Historical examples should be named with their place or period where the source provides it; avoid claiming one alloy has every desirable property.",example:"Adding a controlled amount of carbon to iron produces steel with properties that depend on composition and treatment.",exam:"Name the base material, added element and property being changed."},
      {title:"Mordants and color fixing",notes:"A mordant is a substance used in some dye processes to help color bind to fibres. Dyeing may include cleaning the cloth, preparing the dye bath, treating the fibre, applying color and rinsing or drying. Not every dye uses the same mordant, so describe the specific method taught rather than treating the steps as universal.",example:"A fabric may be treated before dyeing so the color holds more strongly after washing.",exam:"List preparation and application steps and state the mordant's role."},
      {title:"Vastu Shastra as a historical design tradition",notes:"Vastu Shastra refers to texts and traditions concerning the design and organization of buildings and settlements. Its concepts may address orientation, proportion, site and spatial relationships. Treat it as a historical knowledge tradition and distinguish textual principles from claims that require modern structural or environmental evidence.",example:"A comparison can describe a text's orientation principle and separately assess a building's actual ventilation or structure.",exam:"Separate historical description from modern engineering evaluation."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Ayurveda and health frameworks",notes:"Ayurveda is a traditional system with its own concepts of health, routines and bodily balance. In an IKS exam, describe the course's historical framework and examples without presenting it as a substitute for a modern clinical diagnosis. When discussing present-day wellbeing, use evidence-aware language and distinguish cultural history from medical advice.",example:"Describe a daily routine as a traditional practice, then distinguish it from individualized clinical treatment.",exam:"Keep historical description, current evidence and personal health claims separate."},
      {title:"Yoga Sutras and eight-limbed practice",notes:"The eight-limbed framework associated with the Yoga Sutras includes ethical restraints, observances, posture, breath regulation, sensory withdrawal, concentration, meditation and absorption. The sequence shows why yoga cannot be reduced to exercise alone. Use the terms and order taught in your course and explain how practice progresses from conduct toward attention and contemplation.",example:"A simple map can show yama and niyama before posture and breath, followed by inward concentration practices.",exam:"Name the limbs in order and explain the broader aim of the framework."},
      {title:"Dharma as role, duty and ethical judgment",notes:"Dharma can refer to duty, order, conduct or a normative way of acting, depending on context. A literary story may explore conflicts between duties rather than present an easy rule. In an exam, identify the decision, the competing responsibilities and the text's way of resolving or questioning them.",example:"A character may face a tension between a promise to one person and a duty to a wider community.",exam:"Use a specific episode and explain the ethical conflict, not only the word's translation."},
      {title:"Arthashastra and public administration",notes:"The Arthashastra examines administration through institutions and practical concerns such as revenue, officials, law, diplomacy, security and public order. It is a historical source with a particular political setting, not a description of modern constitutional government. Compare one administrative concern carefully and state both the similarity and the historical difference.",example:"Revenue collection can be compared as a governance problem, while the authority and political system differ by period.",exam:"State historical context before drawing a modern comparison."}
    ]
  ],
  math: [
    [
      {title:"Set difference and symmetric difference",notes:"A−B contains elements that are in A but not in B. The symmetric difference A△B contains elements that belong to exactly one of the sets. These operations are useful for comparing groups and checking membership. A Venn diagram makes clear which regions are shaded; remember that A−B is not generally the same as B−A.",example:"A={1,2,3}, B={3,4}: A−B={1,2}; A△B={1,2,4}.",exam:"Write the universe and shade or list the correct regions."},
      {title:"Reflexive, symmetric and transitive tests",notes:"Test each property independently. Reflexive requires every (a,a); symmetric requires the reverse pair whenever (a,b) is present; transitive requires (a,c) whenever (a,b) and (b,c) are present. One counterexample disproves a property. For a finite relation, a matrix or arrow diagram can expose missing pairs quickly.",example:"R={(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(2,2)} on {1,2} is reflexive, symmetric and transitive.",exam:"Use the definition for each property and show a counterexample if it fails."},
      {title:"Equivalence classes as partitions",notes:"If a relation is an equivalence relation, each element's class contains all elements equivalent to it. Classes cover the set and do not overlap unless they are identical, so they form a partition. To find classes, choose a representative and test which elements satisfy the relation; then check that no element is omitted.",example:"Congruence modulo 3 partitions integers into remainders 0, 1 and 2.",exam:"List a representative, its class and explain why the classes partition the set."},
      {title:"Warshall updates with an adjacency matrix",notes:"Warshall's algorithm tests one intermediate vertex at a time. At stage k, M[i,j] becomes 1 if it was already 1 or if i reaches k and k reaches j. The Boolean AND checks a two-part path; OR keeps any known path. Keep a separate matrix after each k for small examples to avoid mixing updates.",example:"If at k=2, M[1,2]=1 and M[2,3]=1, update M[1,3] to 1.",exam:"Show the initial matrix, each intermediate-vertex step and the final reachability meaning."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Pigeonhole principle with a guaranteed minimum",notes:"If N objects are placed into k boxes, at least one box contains at least ceil(N/k) objects. The basic form says k+1 objects in k boxes forces a repeated box. Identify the objects and the categories carefully; the principle guarantees a minimum, not which particular box has it.",example:"Among 13 people and 12 birth months, at least two share a birth month.",exam:"State objects, boxes and the guaranteed count using the ceiling rule."},
      {title:"Combination identities and symmetry",notes:"The number of ways to choose r objects from n is nCr. Choosing the r selected objects or the n−r objects left behind describes the same selection, so nCr=nC(n−r). This identity can simplify calculations and helps check answers. Use factorials carefully, especially when r=0 or r=n.",example:"10C8=10C2=45, since choosing eight leaves two unchosen.",exam:"Explain the selection interpretation, not only the formula."},
      {title:"Recurrence initial conditions and sequence checking",notes:"A recurrence does not fully determine a sequence without enough initial values. After solving its characteristic equation, substitute those values to find constants, then generate the first terms and compare them with the recurrence. This catches algebra errors and confirms indexing such as whether the first term is a₀ or a₁.",example:"For aₙ=2aₙ₋₁ with a₀=3, the terms start 3,6,12,24.",exam:"Write the initial index explicitly and verify at least two terms."},
      {title:"Counting with complementary cases",notes:"Sometimes it is easier to count all outcomes and subtract the cases you do not want. This is the complement rule. It works when the total is clear and excluded cases are easier to count without overlap. Define the sample space and make sure the excluded events are counted exactly once.",example:"The number of 4-bit strings with at least one 1 is 2⁴−1=15; subtract the all-zero string.",exam:"State the total and the excluded set, then justify why subtraction is valid."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Adjacency matrices and graph representation",notes:"An adjacency matrix records which vertex pairs share an edge. For a simple undirected graph it is symmetric; diagonal entries are usually zero unless loops are allowed. The sum of a row gives the vertex degree in a simple graph. A matrix can make path algorithms and computer storage easier than drawing a large network.",example:"For edges A–B and B–C, rows A,B,C are [0,1,0], [1,0,1], [0,1,0].",exam:"Keep vertex order fixed across row and column labels."},
      {title:"Connected components and bridges",notes:"A connected component is a maximal set of vertices joined by paths. A bridge is an edge whose removal increases the number of components. Bridges identify fragile links in transportation or communication networks. To test one, remove it on a sketch and check whether some vertices can no longer reach one another.",example:"In a chain A–B–C, each edge is a bridge; in a triangle, no single edge is a bridge.",exam:"Show the graph before and after removing the candidate edge."},
      {title:"Euler trails and degree conditions",notes:"For a connected undirected graph, an Euler circuit exists when all vertices have even degree. An open Euler trail exists when exactly two vertices have odd degree; those odd-degree vertices are the start and end. Connectivity among non-isolated vertices matters. These criteria are sufficient as well as necessary for the standard case.",example:"Degrees 1,2,2,1 give two odd vertices, so an Euler trail can start at one and end at the other.",exam:"Count degrees, check connectivity and identify the route endpoints."},
      {title:"Graph coloring and chromatic number",notes:"A proper vertex coloring assigns colors so adjacent vertices receive different colors. The chromatic number is the smallest number of colors required. Coloring can model exam timetables or register allocation, where conflicts prevent two tasks from sharing a time or resource. A shown coloring gives an upper bound; proving a minimum also requires a lower-bound argument.",example:"A triangle needs three colors because each pair of its three vertices is adjacent.",exam:"Give a valid coloring and explain why fewer colors cannot work."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Matrix addition, subtraction and multiplication",notes:"Addition and subtraction require matrices of the same dimensions and operate entry by entry. Multiplication AB is defined when the number of columns of A equals the number of rows of B. Each result entry is a row–column dot product, so multiplication order matters and AB need not equal BA.",example:"A 2×3 matrix can multiply a 3×2 matrix, producing a 2×2 result.",exam:"Check dimensions before calculating and show one row–column product."},
      {title:"Elementary row operations and echelon form",notes:"Row operations include swapping rows, multiplying a row by a nonzero constant and adding a multiple of one row to another. They preserve the solution set of a linear system. Echelon form places pivots in a staircase pattern with zeros below each pivot. Reduced echelon form also clears entries above pivots and scales pivots to 1.",example:"R₂←R₂−2R₁ can eliminate the first entry of a second row.",exam:"Annotate every operation and avoid multiplying a row by zero."},
      {title:"Cramer's rule and determinant ratios",notes:"Cramer's rule solves a square linear system when the coefficient determinant D is nonzero. Replace one coefficient column at a time with the constants to form Dₓ, Dᵧ and so on; each variable equals its replacement determinant divided by D. If D=0, the rule does not apply and the system may have no or infinitely many solutions.",example:"For x+y=5, 2x−y=1, D=−3, Dₓ=−6, Dᵧ=−9, so x=2 and y=3.",exam:"Show D first, then each replaced-column determinant and verify the result."},
      {title:"Characteristic polynomial and eigenvalue checks",notes:"The characteristic polynomial det(A−λI) produces eigenvalues as its roots. Repeated roots may or may not have enough independent eigenvectors to form a basis. After finding an eigenvector, substitute it into Av=λv to verify the calculation. Keep λ as a scalar and I as the identity matrix when forming A−λI.",example:"For a diagonal matrix, each diagonal entry is an eigenvalue with its coordinate vector as an eigenvector.",exam:"Check the determinant equation and verify every reported vector is nonzero."}
    ]
  ],
  'problem-solving': [
    [
      {title:"Algorithm invariants and loop reasoning",notes:"A loop invariant is a statement that is true before and after each loop iteration. To use it, show it is true at the start, remains true after one pass and helps prove the final result when the loop ends. This is a structured way to explain correctness for tasks such as summing values or maintaining a sorted prefix.",example:"In a running sum, after i items, total equals the sum of the first i input values.",exam:"State the invariant and connect it to initialization, update and termination."},
      {title:"Pseudocode conventions and traceability",notes:"Pseudocode is language-neutral but should still use consistent indentation, meaningful names and explicit control flow. Each line should be simple enough to trace. Avoid mixing C syntax with vague English unless the question allows it. A reader should be able to identify input, decisions, repetition and output without guessing.",example:"FOR i ← 1 TO n; total ← total + score[i]; END FOR" ,exam:"Use indentation and explicit loop boundaries so the method is unambiguous."},
      {title:"Flowchart decisions and loop exits",notes:"Every decision diamond should have clearly labelled branches, commonly Yes and No. A loop should return to the condition only after the body updates the values that affect it. Show a clear exit path and make sure the stop condition is reachable. This visual review can catch a missing update or a branch that never ends.",example:"A digit loop returns to n>0 after n becomes n/10; the No branch exits to the result.",exam:"Trace both branches and prove the loop can reach its exit."},
      {title:"Time complexity and growth",notes:"Time complexity describes how an algorithm's work grows as input size n grows, ignoring machine-specific details. A single pass is O(n); nested loops over n items may be O(n²); repeatedly halving a range is O(log n). Complexity helps compare scalability but does not by itself prove an algorithm is correct or fastest for tiny inputs.",example:"Binary search uses about log₂n comparisons, while linear search may inspect all n items.",exam:"Count the dominant operations and state what n represents."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Nested loops and execution tables",notes:"A nested loop completes the inner loop for each outer-loop value. Trace both indexes in a table; this reveals how many iterations occur and helps reason about patterns or matrix traversal. If both loops run n times, the total number of inner operations is approximately n². Boundary conditions must prevent index n from being accessed.",example:"For i=0..1 and j=0..2, the pairs are (0,0),(0,1),(0,2),(1,0),(1,1),(1,2).",exam:"List the index pairs and show when each loop increments."},
      {title:"C operators, precedence and conditions",notes:"C evaluates operators according to precedence, and parentheses make the intended grouping explicit. Relational operators produce truth values; logical AND and OR combine conditions, while bitwise operators work on bits. Confusing = with == or using && instead of & changes behavior. Break complex conditions into named steps when tracing them.",example:"Use (marks >= 40 && attendance >= 75) for two conditions that must both hold.",exam:"Add parentheses and explain the condition in ordinary words."},
      {title:"Input validation and safe data handling",notes:"Input validation checks that a value is in the permitted range and in the expected form before it is used. Invalid input should be rejected, requested again or handled according to the specification. In C, also check whether input conversion succeeded when the problem requires robust handling. Validation prevents invalid values from corrupting later calculations.",example:"A menu accepting choices 1–4 should reject 0 and 5 before using the choice in a switch.",exam:"State the valid range and describe what happens when input is outside it."},
      {title:"Loop tracing and off-by-one errors",notes:"Off-by-one errors happen when a loop runs one time too many or too few. Check the initial value, comparison operator and update as a set. For an array of n elements indexed 0 through n−1, a loop usually uses i<n; i<=n reaches an invalid index. Trace n=0, n=1 and a normal case.",example:"for(i=0;i<5;i++) visits 0,1,2,3,4 exactly five times.",exam:"Write the first and last loop values and count how many iterations occur."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Palindrome testing and reverse arithmetic",notes:"To test a numeric palindrome, save the original number, build its reverse with reverse=reverse×10+digit and compare the two values. Decide how to handle negatives and trailing zeroes. A string palindrome test has different rules because it can preserve leading zeroes and compare characters directly.",example:"For 121: reverse becomes 1, then 12, then 121; it equals the saved original.",exam:"Show the digit trace and explain the chosen sign/zero convention."},
      {title:"Factorial recursion and iteration",notes:"Factorial can be computed iteratively with a running product or recursively from n!=n×(n−1)!, with 0!=1. Both methods need a valid domain, usually nonnegative integers. Iteration uses constant extra space; recursion uses call-stack space. The result grows quickly, so choose a data type and input limit that avoid overflow.",example:"5!=5×4×3×2×1=120; 0!=1 by definition.",exam:"State the base case or initialization, update and overflow limitation."},
      {title:"Running averages without storing all values",notes:"A streaming average can be computed using count and sum, so the program need not keep every input value. Initialize both to zero, update them for valid observations and divide only after confirming count>0. For large data, use a type wide enough for the total and consider numeric precision.",example:"Read 6, 8 and 10: count=3, sum=24, average=8.",exam:"Show the update and protect against an empty input stream."},
      {title:"Integer overflow and type selection",notes:"Integer types have finite ranges. Overflow can produce an incorrect result, especially in factorials, digit powers and accumulated totals. Estimate the maximum intermediate value, not just the final displayed value, and choose a suitable type. If the required range exceeds the type, the algorithm needs a different representation or explicit overflow handling.",example:"A small integer type may hold 100, but 100! is far beyond ordinary machine integers.",exam:"Explain the range requirement and state how overflow is prevented or detected."}
    ],
    [
      {title:"Function prototypes, return values and scope",notes:"A prototype gives the compiler the function's signature before a call. A function definition provides the body; local variables exist only within their scope. A return value sends one result back to the caller, while parameters pass inputs. Clear names and small responsibilities make functions easier to test and reuse.",example:"int square(int x); declares a function that accepts an int and returns an int.",exam:"Label return type, function name, parameter types and where the result goes."},
      {title:"Binary search with low, mid and high",notes:"Binary search works on sorted data. Set low and high to the current inclusive range, compute mid safely, compare the middle value with the target and discard the half that cannot contain it. Repeat until found or low>high. Updating low=mid+1 or high=mid−1 prevents repeating the same midpoint.",example:"Search [2,5,8,12,16] for 12: mid=8, keep right half, then find 12.",exam:"Show each low/mid/high update and confirm the array is sorted."},
      {title:"Selection sort passes and invariant",notes:"At pass i, selection sort finds the smallest value from positions i through n−1 and swaps it into position i. After each pass, the prefix through i is sorted and contains the smallest values. The algorithm uses O(n²) comparisons and generally performs few swaps. Trace the array after each pass to avoid confusing selection with adjacent swapping.",example:"[4,2,3] → choose 2 and swap → [2,4,3] → choose 3 → [2,3,4].",exam:"Show the selected minimum and array after every pass."},
      {title:"Two-dimensional arrays and row-column indexing",notes:"A two-dimensional C array is stored as rows of elements, accessed with two indexes such as a[row][column]. Both indexes begin at zero and must stay within their respective bounds. Nested loops commonly visit row first and column second. For matrix operations, dimensions and index meaning should be stated before the code.",example:"In int a[2][3], valid row indexes are 0–1 and column indexes are 0–2.",exam:"Label dimensions as rows×columns and check both bounds in the loops."}
    ]
  ]
};
